# Homebase - Product Requirements Document

## Overview

Homebase is a Chrome extension that serves as a personal productivity tool combining tab management, bookmark organization, note-taking, and an AI-powered assistant. The AI agent acts as a personal organizer, search engine, and contextual helper that can understand and work with your browsing context, notes, and resources.

## Vision

A minimal, personal tool that helps organize digital work by intelligently managing tabs, bookmarks, and notes while providing an AI assistant that understands context across browsing sessions, projects, and resources.

### Strategic direction (reconciled)

- **Data stays local** (IndexedDB + existing backup story); **AI is optional** and uses **user-provided API keys** (cloud LLM first). No required server.
- **Longer arc** (see [`OVERVIEW.md`](OVERVIEW.md)): bulk/manual bookmark import → optional site capture → embeddings/RAG (API or later in-browser) → **gated** agentic research → workspaces/study paths → per-page / same-site relational notes.
- **Near-term execution order** (aligned with [`BACKLOG.md`](BACKLOG.md)): **(1)** AI API infrastructure + minimal UI, **(2)** manual bulk bookmark import at scale (preview + **commit** + perf), **(3)** AI grounded on bookmarks with citations + **auto-categorization / enrichment** as next product slices, **(4)** further steps TBD.

## Target Audience

**Primary**: Personal use by the creator
**Secondary**: Potential sharing with select individuals for feedback
**Future**: May evolve into open-source project or MVP based on interest

## Core Principles

- **Personal Tool First**: Built for personal use, not optimized for public release
- **Client-Side Processing**: No server required; AI calls made directly from client
- **Local-First**: Data stored locally with optional cloud sync/backup
- **Minimal & Simple**: Focus on core functionality, avoid over-engineering
- **Privacy-Focused**: All data stays local; cloud sync is optional

## Key Features

### 1. Tab Management ✅ (Implemented)
- ✅ View and organize open tabs across windows (Tab Commander)
- ✅ Multi-window handling with compact navigator (W1, W2, W3)
- ✅ Tab search and filtering
- ✅ Drag & drop tabs between windows
- ✅ Multi-select tabs and windows
- ✅ "Open Here" preview tabs (avoids desktop switching)
- ✅ List/Gallery view switcher
- ✅ Resizable/collapsible panel
- ⏳ Group tabs by project or context (pending Projects implementation)
- ⏳ Quick access to tab collections (partially via Workspaces)

### 2. Bookmark Management ✅ (Implemented)
- ✅ Enhanced bookmark organization
- ✅ Categorization (Collections) and tagging
- ✅ Add/Edit/Delete bookmarks
- ✅ Manual entry (dashboard + side panel)
- ✅ Save current tab to bookmark
- ✅ Grid/list view options in dashboard
- ✅ Bookmarks can be created from dashboard with project/collection selection
- ✅ Inline creation of project/collection from bookmark/note create dialog
- ✅ **Import Studio** (Bookmarks → Import): **file** ingest (Netscape HTML, CSV, JSON; content-based format detection; JSON-in-`.js`/`.txt`), **X bookmarks export** shape, **Chrome `bookmarks` API**, unified **preview**, optional destination, **`Commit to DB`** with dedupe/merge via **`bulkImportBookmarks`** (batch IndexedDB transaction for large imports). CSV supports **Raindrop**-like columns (`title`, `url`, `note`, `tags`, `folder`/`collection`, **`cover`**, etc.). **Description** carried on import candidates (stored via notes/placement for now). AI-assistant tab remains **mock** only.
- Next: persist **cover** + structured **import provenance** on `Item.metadata`; folder→collection mapping; **scale/backup** review for very large libraries; **enrichment** (fetch/X threads) and **AI auto-categorization** (see [`BACKLOG.md`](BACKLOG.md)).

### 3. Workspaces ✅ (Implemented - Beyond Original Spec)
- ✅ Save all windows, one window, or multiple tabs
- ✅ Restore all, selected, or single windows
- ✅ Two-column detail view (windows/tabs)
- ✅ Workspace list with cards
- ✅ Full CRUD operations
- ✅ When updating an existing snapshot from Tab Commander, **append** new links with **URL deduplication** (normalized URL), rather than replacing the workspace contents outright

### 4. Note Management 🟡 (Partially Implemented)
- ✅ Notes can be created manually in dashboard
- ✅ Notes are organized through the same project/collection structure as bookmarks
- ✅ Notes are currently represented as `items` with empty URL (URL optional)
- ⏳ First-class `notes` store is present in schema but not yet the primary UI model
- ⏳ Page-context note capture still pending

### 5. AI Agent Assistant 🟡 (Infra Started)

#### Core Capabilities
- **Personal Assistant**: Help organize and manage tabs, bookmarks, and notes
- **Search Engine**: Provide predictable scoped library retrieval with default-AND terms, exact phrases, OR/AND/`+`, exclusions, and domain guards. Optional embeddings rank exact matches and surface separately labelled semantic relations without weakening hard query or organization boundaries.
- **Project Organizer**: Help organize notes and resources around specific projects
- **Contextual Helper**: Answer questions while reading pages with full context awareness

#### Context Awareness
The AI agent can access:
- Current page content and context
- Project-related notes and resources
- Bookmarks and saved links
- Previous notes and conversations
- Browser history context (if available)

#### Example Use Cases
- "Provide resources for learning how to use TradingView" (specific or general)
- "Organize my notes about [project name]"
- "What did I note about [topic]?"
- "Find related resources to what I'm reading"
- "Help me understand this page in context of my [project]"

#### Current Infra Baseline (shipped)
- ✅ Pluggable AI client module under `src/lib/ai` with provider adapter boundary
- ✅ OpenRouter-compatible chat/completions adapter (OpenAI-style request shape)
- ✅ Optional Chrome native/on-device provider path (graceful fallback when unsupported)
- ✅ Settings UI for provider, model id, base URL, API key, timeout, temperature, and output token cap
- ✅ Strict model-id match option + task-based routing scaffold (`single` or `by-task`)
- ✅ Test prompt surface in Settings with visible provider-returned model id
- ✅ API key persisted in `chrome.storage.local` (not backup JSON), plus in-app storage/security note
- ✅ Bookmarks view “Ask AI” starter over current filtered scope with source refs (`[B1]`, `[B2]`, ...)

### 6. Resource Linking ⏳ (Planned)
- ⏳ Link bookmarks to notes
- ⏳ Link notes to projects
- ✅ Create resource collections (Collections exist)
- ⏳ Visualize relationships between notes, bookmarks, and projects

## Technical Architecture

### Frontend
- **Framework**: React + TypeScript
- **Build Tool**: Vite
- **UI**: Minimal, clean interface focused on functionality

### Data Storage
- **Primary**: ✅ Local IndexedDB (via idb library) - Implemented
- **Export/Import**: ✅ JSON backup/restore - Implemented
- **Sync/Backup**: ⏳ Optional Dropbox API integration (Planned)
  - Sync notes, bookmarks, and project data
  - Backup functionality
  - Cross-device access (future)

### AI Integration
- **Architecture**: Direct API calls from client-side
- **Processing**: Client-side processing of AI responses
- **API Key**: Stored in extension `chrome.storage.local` for no-friction usage (not included in DB backup JSON)
- **Endpoint baseline**: OpenRouter (`/chat/completions` OpenAI-compatible shape); adapter boundary allows additional providers later

### Chrome Extension
- **Manifest**: Chrome Extension Manifest V3 (`public/manifest.json`)
- **Service Worker**: `service-worker.js` — lazy contextual side-panel visibility created only from toolbar gestures; install/startup/reload never mutate native panel state; dashboard and `chrome://extensions` tabs remain excluded (extension management opens/focuses the dashboard instead); `focus-tab` message helper for dashboard activation
- **New Tab**: `chrome_url_overrides.newtab` → extension `index.html` so new tabs open Homebase directly
- **Known limitation**: Chrome may show a persistent browser/extension footer on overridden new-tab pages; **not** reliably removable for extension-hosted UI. A hosted web dashboard would be the escape hatch if a chrome-free full viewport is required later.
- **Content Scripts**: Not currently declared in manifest; future page-context capture would add them when implemented.
- **Storage**: Chrome `storage` where needed + IndexedDB for app data

## Data Model

### Current Implementation Status

**✅ Implemented:**
- **Projects**: `{ id, name, description?, isDefault, created_at, updated_at }`
- **Collections**: `{ id, name, color, isDefault, primaryProjectId, projectIds[], created_at, updated_at }`
- **Items (Bookmarks/Notes)**: `{ id, url, title, favicon?, collectionIds[], tags[], notes?, source, created_at, updated_at }`
- **Workspaces**: `{ id, name, projectId?, windows[], created_at, updated_at }` - Snapshots of browser sessions
- **Snapshots**: Historical tab snapshots

**🟡 In transition:**
- **Notes store**: first-class `notes` object store exists in DB/export/import, but dashboard Notes UI currently uses note-like `items` (empty URL).

### Projects ✅ (Implemented)
- Project name/description, default project handling
- Virtual "All" project aggregate view in UI
- Project owns collections through `primaryProjectId` / `projectIds[]`
- Per-project default "Unsorted" collection creation and maintenance

### Notes 🟡 (Current behavior)
- Notes are currently modeled as `items` with no URL (and optional notes text)
- Notes are project/collection-scoped through `collectionIds[]`
- Dedicated `notes` store exists but is not yet the primary notes UI path

### Bookmarks ✅ (Implemented as Items)
- ✅ URL
- ✅ Title
- ✅ Description/notes (as `notes` field)
- ✅ Categories/tags (as `tags[]` array)
- ✅ Collection assignment (via `collectionIds[]`)
- ✅ Project association via collection membership
- ✅ Bookmark-vs-note UI separation (`bookmark = URL`, `note = no URL`)
- ⏳ Screenshot/preview (optional - future enhancement)

### Tabs/Windows ✅ (Managed via Chrome APIs + Workspaces)
- ✅ Tab metadata (via Chrome APIs)
- ✅ Window grouping (via Workspaces)
- ✅ Collections/sessions (via Workspaces)

## User Interface

### Main Views
1. **Dashboard**: ✅ IDE-style shell — left sidebar (**project dropdown**, collections-in-scope, Content/Tools); middle **list pane + item tabs** for bookmarks/notes/workspaces (tabs persist across scope; **drag-and-drop** reorder; aggregate **Open as tab** with scoped titles + **list/grid**; optional **Common tab** stacking sections by project/collection); **full-page** Tab Commander & Settings; right assistant panel. Deletes support **remove from collection** vs **delete everywhere** where placement context exists. See [`UI_IDE_REDESIGN.md`](UI_IDE_REDESIGN.md).
2. **Tab Commander**: ✅ Full-page live tab management; themed consistently with dashboard tokens (`BottomPanel`).
3. **Bookmarks View**: ✅ Scoped list + tabbed detail; search/import in list header; in-tab edit; bookmark-grounded AI from filtered scope.
4. **Workspaces View**: ✅ Scoped list; workspace opens as **one tab** with link list and bulk actions (open/bookmark/remove).
5. **Collections View**: ✅ Dedicated collections + items workspace
6. **Projects View**: ✅ Implemented project workspace and cards
7. **Notes View**: ✅ Implemented note-focused view over note-like items
8. **AI Assistant**: 🟡 Infra/settings + test prompt + first bookmark-grounded ask flow implemented; richer citation/selection UX still pending
9. **Side Panel**: ✅ Bookmark-centric workflow — URL/title prefill from active tab (updates on tab change); notes field; project/collection selection with inline create; list of saved copies for current URL (edit, placement-aware remove, **add new copy** with explicit project/collection choice); switching to a collection that already contains the URL surfaces that copy’s notes; duplicate URL blocked within same collection; Open Dashboard; Set Homebase as Home (manual Chrome startup/home setup). **BroadcastChannel** / visibility patterns refresh lists when the dashboard mutates data. Backup onboarding/import flows remain dashboard-first when required.

### Design Philosophy
- Clean and minimal
- Focus on functionality over aesthetics
- Easy navigation between views
- Quick access to common actions

## Development Phases

### Phase 1: Core Foundation ✅ (Mostly Complete)
- ✅ Basic tab management (Tab Commander)
- ✅ Bookmark organization (Collections + Items)
- ✅ Workspaces (beyond original spec)
- ✅ Local database setup (IndexedDB **v4** — placements + URL dedup)
- ✅ Export/Import functionality
- 🟡 Notes store/UI unification decision (next priority)
- ✅ Projects implementation

### Phase 1.5: Data Model & Linking (Current Focus)
- ✅ Define and implement Projects ↔ Collections core relationship
- ✅ **Items v4**: canonical URL dedup + **placements** (per-collection notes/tags); merge on save; granular remove-from-collection vs delete-item — see [`DATA_MODEL_DEDUP.md`](DATA_MODEL_DEDUP.md)
- 🟡 Align top-level UI flows with the model (project/collection-aware create flows) — **partially addressed** by IDE iteration 1 scope UX + side-panel copy flows
- ⏳ Decide and implement single long-term Notes model (`items` note-like vs first-class `notes` store)
- ⏳ Implement richer resource linking UX (bookmarks ↔ notes ↔ projects)

### Phase 2: AI Integration (In progress — phased)

- **2a — Infra (first)**: ✅ Initial slice shipped (Settings + persisted config + HTTP client + test prompt + model visibility). Remaining: stricter routing/validation and additional provider adapters as needed.
- **2b — Bookmarks at scale**: Import Studio **preview + commit** shipped (HTML/CSV/JSON + Chrome API + **X export adapter**, content auto-detect, **batch** `bulkImportBookmarks`). **Remaining:** cover + import **metadata**, folder→collection mapping, **very-large-library** scale/backup strategy, **enrichment** (X threads / fetch), **AI clustering** into collections/projects (see [`BACKLOG.md`](BACKLOG.md)).
- **2c — Bookmark-grounded AI**: 🟡 Starter shipped (filtered-scope ask + source refs). Next: selected-item scope, richer source linking chips, and lightweight assist actions (summary/tags).
- **2d — Later**: Embeddings + retrieval; optional in-browser embeddings; content scripts for page context; agentic tool loops with budgets.

### Phase 3: Advanced Features (Planned)
- Resource linking visualization
- Dropbox sync integration
- Enhanced AI capabilities
- Cross-context awareness

### Phase 4: Polish & Sharing (If Desired)
- Landing page
- Screenshots and demo videos
- Documentation
- Potential open-source release

## Non-Requirements (Out of Scope)

- Multi-user support
- Public API
- Server infrastructure
- Advanced security features (beyond basic)
- Public distribution/Chrome Web Store (initially)
- Real-time collaboration
- Mobile app

## Success Metrics (Personal Use)

- Daily active use
- Number of notes created
- Number of projects organized
- AI assistant helpfulness
- Time saved in organization
- Interest from others (if shared)

## Future Considerations

- Open-source release
- MVP for broader audience
- Additional AI providers
- Enhanced sync capabilities
- Browser extension for other browsers
- Mobile companion (if needed)

## Notes

- **Extension naming**: Manifest/marketing name may read “Homebase” while docs refer to “Homebase”; align naming when preparing store listing.
- This is a personal tool; priorities may shift based on actual usage
- Focus on building something useful first, optimize later
- Keep it simple and maintainable
- Security is not a primary concern for personal use, but basic best practices should be followed

